    Section(
      title: '+ What serological and electrodiagnostic tests would you order to confirm MG? Why is CT chest essential?',
      content:
          '**Serological Testing**:\n1. **Acetylcholine receptor (AChR) antibodies**: Positive in **85% of generalized MG** and **50% of ocular MG**. This is the first-line serological test. Includes binding, blocking, and modulating antibodies.\n2. **Muscle-specific kinase (MuSK) antibodies**: Order if AChR antibodies are negative. Positive in **40-50% of AChR-seronegative** patients. MuSK-positive MG has distinct clinical features (prominent bulbar and facial weakness, muscle atrophy).\n3. **LRP4 antibodies**: Newer test for double-seronegative MG (both AChR and MuSK negative). Available at specialized labs.\n4. **Striational (anti-striated muscle) antibodies**: If positive, increases concern for **thymoma** (present in 75-85% of thymoma-associated MG).\n\n**Electrodiagnostic Studies (EDX)**:\n1. **Repetitive nerve stimulation (RNS)**: Low-frequency stimulation (2-3 Hz) of a motor nerve while recording CMAP. A **decremental response of ≥10%** between the first and fourth/fifth response is diagnostic. Test proximal muscles (spinal accessory-trapezius, facial nerve-nasalis) for higher sensitivity in ocular/bulbar MG. Sensitivity: 75% for generalized MG, only 30-50% for ocular MG.\n2. **Single-fiber EMG (SFEMG)**: Most sensitive test for MG (sensitivity 92-99%). Measures **jitter** (variability in time interval between firing of two muscle fibers in the same motor unit). Increased jitter and **blocking** are characteristic. Performed in orbicularis oculi for ocular MG or extensor digitorum communis for generalized.\n\n**CT Chest (with contrast)**:\n- **Essential** in all newly diagnosed MG patients to evaluate for **thymoma**.\n- **10-15%** of MG patients have thymoma; conversely, **30-50%** of thymoma patients develop MG.\n- **Thymic hyperplasia** is present in **60-70%** of AChR-positive MG patients (especially younger women).\n- Thymoma is an indication for **thymectomy regardless of MG severity**.\n- If CT is equivocal, MRI chest can provide better characterization.\n\n**Additional Workup**:\n- TSH (thyroid disease coexists in 10-15% of MG patients — she already has hypothyroidism)\n- ANA, RF (screen for coexisting autoimmune diseases)\n- Baseline CBC, CMP, LFTs (before starting immunosuppression)\n- Pulmonary function tests with NIF and FVC as baseline',
