      content:
          '**Pharmacologic Management (Stepwise Approach)**:\n\n1. **Pyridostigmine (Mestinon)**: First-line symptomatic treatment.\n- Starting dose: 30 mg TID, titrate to 60 mg every 4-6 hours as needed (max 120 mg every 4 hours)\n- Mechanism: Acetylcholinesterase inhibitor — increases ACh availability at the NMJ\n- Side effects: Cholinergic (diarrhea, cramping, increased secretions, bradycardia). Use glycopyrrolate for GI side effects.\n- **Cholinergic crisis**: Overdosing causes weakness PLUS excessive secretions, miosis, bradycardia — distinguish from myasthenic crisis.\n\n2. **Corticosteroids**: If pyridostigmine alone insufficient.\n- Prednisone starting at 10-20 mg daily, gradually increase to 40-60 mg daily\n- **Caution**: Rapid initiation of high-dose steroids can cause transient worsening — must start low and titrate slowly in an outpatient setting or initiate in hospital if bulbar/respiratory symptoms present\n- Taper once remission achieved; chronic low-dose may be needed\n\n3. **Steroid-sparing immunosuppressants** (add if steroid-dependent or intolerant):\n- **Mycophenolate mofetil** 1000 mg BID or **Azathioprine** 2-3 mg/kg/day (check TPMT before starting)\n- Onset of benefit: 6-12 months for azathioprine, 6 months for mycophenolate\n- Monitoring: CBC and LFTs every 1-3 months\n\n4. **Rescue therapies** (for exacerbations or crisis):\n- **IVIG** (2 g/kg over 2-5 days) or **Plasmapheresis** (5 exchanges over 10-14 days)\n- Used for myasthenic crisis, pre-operative optimization, or severe exacerbations\n\n5. **Newer agents**: Eculizumab (complement inhibitor), efgartigimod (FcRn inhibitor) — for refractory generalized AChR-positive MG.\n\n**Exercise and Rehabilitation Guidelines**:\n- **Exercise is safe and beneficial** in stable MG when properly supervised\n- **Aerobic exercise**: Low-to-moderate intensity, 20-30 minutes, 3-5 times/week. Avoid exercising in extreme heat.\n- **Resistance training**: Low-to-moderate intensity, avoid exhaustive exercise. Use RPE scale (keep at 12-14/20). Rest between sets.\n- **Timing**: Exercise in the morning when strength is best (after pyridostigmine dose has taken effect, typically 1-2 hours after dosing)\n- **Monitoring**: Teach patient to recognize signs of overexertion (increased ptosis, difficulty swallowing, voice changes). Stop exercise if these occur.\n- **Pulmonary rehabilitation**: Inspiratory muscle training may improve respiratory function\n- **Avoid**: Extreme heat exposure, exhaustive exercise, exercise late in the day when symptoms peak',
