          '**Medications to AVOID or Use with Extreme Caution in MG**:\n\n1. **Antibiotics**: Aminoglycosides (gentamicin, tobramycin), fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin), macrolides (azithromycin) — all can worsen neuromuscular transmission\n2. **Cardiac medications**: Beta-blockers (propranolol, metoprolol), calcium channel blockers (verapamil), procainamide, quinidine\n3. **Neuromuscular blocking agents**: Succinylcholine (prolonged paralysis), non-depolarizing agents (exaggerated response — use only with careful monitoring)\n4. **Other**: Magnesium sulfate (blocks NMJ transmission — dangerous), D-penicillamine (can induce MG), botulinum toxin, quinine, statins (rare reports of worsening), immune checkpoint inhibitors (pembrolizumab, nivolumab — can trigger or worsen MG)\n\n**Patient should carry a medical alert card/bracelet listing the MG diagnosis and these precautions.**\n\n**MG Crisis Prevention and Management**:\n\n**Myasthenic Crisis Definition**: Respiratory failure requiring intubation/mechanical ventilation due to MG exacerbation. Mortality 3-5% with modern ICU care.\n\n**Prevention**:\n- Regular monitoring of NIF and FVC (crisis risk increases when FVC <15-20 mL/kg or NIF weaker than -20 cmH2O)\n- Medication adherence counseling\n- Avoid known triggers: infection, surgery, medication changes, emotional stress, heat\n- Pre-operative IVIG/PLEX for any planned surgery\n- Annual influenza and pneumococcal vaccination (avoid live vaccines if on immunosuppression)\n\n**Management Protocol (20/30/40 Rule)**:\n- FVC <20 mL/kg — consider ICU admission\n- NIF weaker than -30 cmH2O — prepare for possible intubation\n- FVC declining >30% from baseline — strong indication for intubation\n\n**Treatment of Crisis**:\n1. ICU admission with serial respiratory monitoring (NIF, FVC every 4-6 hours)\n2. **Plasmapheresis** or **IVIG** — both equally effective; PLEX may have faster onset\n3. Hold or reduce pyridostigmine (excess cholinergic secretions complicate